# Cloudflare Pages / Netlify cache headers.
#
# The defaults of most static hosts cache every file aggressively, which is
# usually what you want — EXCEPT for the three URLs below, where stale
# responses make the entire auto-update mechanism fail.
#
# - /sw.js is what the browser polls to discover new Service Workers. If
#   it's HTTP-cached for a day, the browser won't see a new build for a day.
# - /version.json is what the runtime watchdog polls. Must be live.
# - /recover.html is the standalone reset page. Always fresh.
# - /index.html — the SPA shell. Letting it be aggressively cached at the
#   edge means users see yesterday's UI even after the SW redeploys.
#
# Hashed assets (/assets/*.js, /assets/*.css, etc.) can keep their default
# long cache because the filename changes on every build.

/sw.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/workbox-*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/version.json
  Cache-Control: no-store, no-cache, must-revalidate

/recover.html
  Cache-Control: no-store, no-cache, must-revalidate

/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
